<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahakaccana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 5th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Mahakatyayana, Maha Kaccana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahakaccana was one of the leading disciples of the Buddha, praised in the Pali texts as foremost in explaining briefly stated teachings in detail. A native of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>, he is said to have spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in Avanti, and in the Madhura Sutta he teaches King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avantiputra">Avantiputra</a> of Surasena that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> confers no spiritual superiority. Later tradition linked him with the Abhidharma.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahakaccana.jpg" alt="Mahakaccana" width="97" height="195" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kasennen_mahakaccana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
